VeChain application : Common Technical Specifications
=======================================================
Totient Labs <info@totientlabs.com>
Ledger Firmware Team <hello@ledger.fr>
Application version 1.0 - 9th of June 2018

## 1.0
  - Initial release

## About

This application describes the APDU messages interface to communicate with the VeChain application.

The application covers the following functionalities :

  - Retrieve a public VeChain address given a BIP 32 path
  - Sign a basic VeChain transaction given a BIP 32 path
  - Provide callbacks to validate the data associated to an VeChain transaction

The application interface can be accessed over HID or BLE

## General purpose APDUs

### GET VET PUBLIC ADDRESS

#### Description

This command returns the public key and VeChain address for the given BIP 32 path.

The address can be optionally checked on the device before being returned.

#### Coding

'Command'

[width="80%"]
|==============================================================================================================================
| *CLA* | *INS*  | *P1*               | *P2*       | *Lc*     | *Le*
|   E0  |   02   |  00 : return address

                    01 : display address and confirm before returning
                                      |   00 : do not return the chain code

                                          01 : return the chain code | variable | variable
|==============================================================================================================================

'Input data'

[width="80%"]
|==============================================================================================================================
| *Description*                                                                     | *Length*
| Number of BIP 32 derivations to perform (max 10)                                  | 1
| First derivation index (big endian)                                               | 4
| ...                                                                               | 4
| Last derivation index (big endian)                                                | 4
|==============================================================================================================================

'Output data'

[width="80%"]
|==============================================================================================================================
| *Description*                                                                     | *Length*
| Public Key length                                                                 | 1
| Uncompressed Public Key                                                           | var
| VeChain address length                                                            | 1
| VeChain address                                                                   | var
| Chain code if requested                                                           | 32
|==============================================================================================================================


### SIGN VET TRANSACTION

#### Description

This command signs a VeChain transaction after having the user validate the following parameters

  - Gas price
  - Gas limit
  - Recipient address
  - Value

The input data is the RLP encoded transaction (as per https://gitlab.vechain.com/vechain/thor.js/thorjs-tx/blob/master/fields.js), without signature present, streamed to the device in 255 bytes maximum data chunks.

#### Coding

'Command'

[width="80%"]
|==============================================================================================================================
| *CLA* | *INS*  | *P1*               | *P2*       | *Lc*     | *Le*
|   E0  |   04   |  00 : first transaction data block

                    80 : subsequent transaction data block
                                      |   00 | variable | variable
|==============================================================================================================================

'Input data (first transaction data block)'

[width="80%"]
|==============================================================================================================================
| *Description*                                                                     | *Length*
| Number of BIP 32 derivations to perform (max 10)                                  | 1
| First derivation index (big endian)                                               | 4
| ...                                                                               | 4
| Last derivation index (big endian)                                                | 4
| RLP transaction chunk                                                             | variable
|==============================================================================================================================

'Input data (other transaction data block)'

[width="80%"]
|==============================================================================================================================
| *Description*                                                                     | *Length*
| RLP transaction chunk                                                             | variable
|==============================================================================================================================


'Output data'

[width="80%"]
|==============================================================================================================================
| *Description*                                                                     | *Length*
| signature (r + s + v)                                                             | 65
|==============================================================================================================================



### SIGN VET PERSONAL MESSAGE

#### Description

This command signs an [VeChain message](https://github.com/vechain/VIPs/blob/master/vips/VIP-190.md) after having the user validate the BLAKE2B-256 hash of the message being signed.

The input data is the message to sign, streamed to the device in 255 bytes maximum data chunks

#### Coding

'Command'

[width="80%"]
|==============================================================================================================================
| *CLA* | *INS*  | *P1*               | *P2*       | *Lc*     | *Le*
|   E0  |   08   |  00 : first message data block

                    80 : subsequent message data block
                                      |   00       | variable | variable
|==============================================================================================================================

'Input data (first message data block)'

[width="80%"]
|==============================================================================================================================
| *Description*                                                                     | *Length*
| Number of BIP 32 derivations to perform (max 10)                                  | 1
| First derivation index (big endian)                                               | 4
| ...                                                                               | 4
| Last derivation index (big endian)                                                | 4
| Message chunk                                                                     | variable
|==============================================================================================================================

'Input data (other transaction data block)'

[width="80%"]
|==============================================================================================================================
| *Description*                                                                     | *Length*
| Message chunk                                                                     | variable
|==============================================================================================================================


'Output data'

[width="80%"]
|==============================================================================================================================
| *Description*                                                                     | *Length*
| v                                                                                 | 1
| r                                                                                 | 32
| s                                                                                 | 32
|==============================================================================================================================


### GET APP CONFIGURATION

#### Description

This command returns specific application configuration

#### Coding

'Command'

[width="80%"]
|==============================================================================================================================
| *CLA* | *INS*  | *P1*               | *P2*       | *Lc*     | *Le*
|   E0  |   06   |  00                |   00       | 00       | 04
|==============================================================================================================================

'Input data'

None

'Output data'

[width="80%"]
|==============================================================================================================================
| *Description*                                                                     | *Length*
| Flags
        0x01 : arbitrary data signature enabled by user

        0x02 : multi-clause transactions enabled by user

        0x04 : EIP-712 typed-data signing enabled by user (master switch)

        0x08 : EIP-712 v0 (blind signing) enabled by user
                                                                                    | 01
| Application major version                                                         | 01
| Application minor version                                                         | 01
| Application patch version                                                         | 01
|==============================================================================================================================


### SIGN VET EIP 712

#### Description

This command signs a typed-data structured message following the EIP-712
specification (https://eips.ethereum.org/EIPS/eip-712).

The command supports two modes:

  - *v0 (blind sign, P2 = 0x00)*: the host pre-computes the
    `domainSeparator` (32 bytes) and `hashStruct(message)` (32 bytes), and
    sends them along with the BIP32 path. The device computes the final
    digest `keccak256(0x19 || 0x01 || domainSeparator || hashStruct)` and
    displays both 32-byte hashes truncated as `abcd...ef01` for the user to
    review. The user must explicitly accept a "Blind signing" warning since
    the underlying message contents cannot be displayed.

  - *v1 (clear sign, P2 = 0x01)*: the host streams every struct definition
    via `EIP712 SEND STRUCT DEFINITION` and every value via `EIP712 SEND
    STRUCT IMPLEMENTATION` (in this order: first the `EIP712Domain`, then
    the primary type). The device computes both `domainSeparator` and
    `hashStruct(message)` on-device and shows the user every message field
    value individually before signing. This APDU is the last of the flow.

The user must enable the `EIP-712 signing` setting in the in-app menu before
either mode can be used. When the setting is OFF all four EIP-712 INS values
return `0x6985` (Conditions not satisfied).

#### Coding

'Command'

[width="80%"]
|==============================================================================================================================
| *CLA* | *INS*  | *P1*               | *P2*       | *Lc*     | *Le*
|   E0  |   0C   |  00                | 00 : v0

                                        01 : v1
                                                  | variable | variable
|==============================================================================================================================

'Input data (v0, P2 = 0x00)'

[width="80%"]
|==============================================================================================================================
| *Description*                                                                     | *Length*
| Number of BIP 32 derivations to perform (max 10)                                  | 1
| First derivation index (big endian)                                               | 4
| ...                                                                               | 4
| Last derivation index (big endian)                                                | 4
| Domain separator                                                                  | 32
| Message hashStruct                                                                | 32
|==============================================================================================================================

'Input data (v1, P2 = 0x01)'

[width="80%"]
|==============================================================================================================================
| *Description*                                                                     | *Length*
| Number of BIP 32 derivations to perform (max 10)                                  | 1
| First derivation index (big endian)                                               | 4
| ...                                                                               | 4
| Last derivation index (big endian)                                                | 4
|==============================================================================================================================

'Output data'

[width="80%"]
|==============================================================================================================================
| *Description*                                                                     | *Length*
| signature (r + s + v)                                                             | 65
|==============================================================================================================================


### EIP712 SEND STRUCT DEFINITION

#### Description

Registers a struct definition with the device. The encoding is identical to
the one used by `LedgerHQ/app-ethereum`: the first APDU per struct sets its
name (`P2 = 0x00`), then one or more APDUs add field definitions (`P2 =
0xFF`). Each field carries a `TypeDesc` byte plus optional name/size/array
metadata. Refer to the `LedgerHQ/app-ethereum` documentation for the
TypeDesc bit layout.

#### Coding

'Command'

[width="80%"]
|==============================================================================================================================
| *CLA* | *INS*  | *P1*               | *P2*       | *Lc*     | *Le*
|   E0  |   1A   |  00                | 00 : struct name

                                        FF : struct field
                                                  | variable | 00
|==============================================================================================================================


### EIP712 SEND STRUCT IMPLEMENTATION

#### Description

Streams the values of the current root struct. The first APDU per root must
set the struct name with `P2 = 0x00`. The following APDUs push values, one
field at a time, in the order declared by the struct definition.

For atomic and dynamic types each field APDU body is a 2-byte big-endian
length followed by the raw value bytes (`P2 = 0xFF`, `P1 = 0x00` for full
chunks, `P1 = 0x01` for partial chunks of a dynamic value).

For nested custom struct fields, the host first emits a `P2 = 0x00` ROOT
APDU with the inner struct name, then streams its fields, then resumes the
parent struct.

For array fields (`P2 = 0x0F`) the host first emits an APDU whose body is
the array element count (1 byte), then streams that many elements
recursively. The device pushes a fresh keccak256 context onto the hash
stack at the array boundary and pops it when the count is reached, feeding
the array hash back into the parent context.

The first invocation must set the root to `EIP712Domain`; the second
invocation must set the root to the typed-data primary type. After the last
field of the primary type is processed the device is ready to receive the
SIGN APDU (P2 = 0x01).

#### Coding

'Command'

[width="80%"]
|==============================================================================================================================
| *CLA* | *INS*  | *P1*                       | *P2*       | *Lc*     | *Le*
|   E0  |   1C   |  00 : complete send

                    01 : partial send, more to come
                                              | 00 : root struct

                                                FF : struct field
                                                            | variable | 00
|==============================================================================================================================


## Transport protocol

### General transport description

Ledger APDUs requests and responses are encapsulated using a flexible protocol allowing to fragment large payloads over different underlying transport mechanisms.

The common transport header is defined as follows :

[width="80%"]
|==============================================================================================================================
| *Description*                                                                     | *Length*
| Communication channel ID (big endian)                                             | 2
| Command tag                                                                       | 1
| Packet sequence index (big endian)                                                | 2
| Payload                                                                           | var
|==============================================================================================================================

The Communication channel ID allows commands multiplexing over the same physical link. It is not used for the time being, and should be set to 0101 to avoid compatibility issues with implementations ignoring a leading 00 byte.

The Command tag describes the message content. Use TAG_APDU (0x05) for standard APDU payloads, or TAG_PING (0x02) for a simple link test.

The Packet sequence index describes the current sequence for fragmented payloads. The first fragment index is 0x00.

### APDU Command payload encoding

APDU Command payloads are encoded as follows :

[width="80%"]
|==============================================================================================================================
| *Description*                                                                     | *Length*
| APDU length (big endian)                                                          | 2
| APDU CLA                                                                          | 1
| APDU INS                                                                          | 1
| APDU P1                                                                           | 1
| APDU P2                                                                           | 1
| APDU length                                                                       | 1
| Optional APDU data                                                                | var
|==============================================================================================================================

APDU payload is encoded according to the APDU case

[width="80%"]
|=======================================================================================
| Case Number  | *Lc* | *Le* | Case description
|   1          |  0   |  0   | No data in either direction - L is set to 00
|   2          |  0   |  !0  | Input Data present, no Output Data - L is set to Lc
|   3          |  !0  |  0   | Output Data present, no Input Data - L is set to Le
|   4          |  !0  |  !0  | Both Input and Output Data are present - L is set to Lc
|=======================================================================================

### APDU Response payload encoding

APDU Response payloads are encoded as follows :

[width="80%"]
|==============================================================================================================================
| *Description*                                                                     | *Length*
| APDU response length (big endian)                                                 | 2
| APDU response data and Status Word                                                | var
|==============================================================================================================================

### USB mapping

Messages are exchanged with the dongle over HID endpoints over interrupt transfers, with each chunk being 64 bytes long. The HID Report ID is ignored.

### BLE mapping

A similar encoding is used over BLE, without the Communication channel ID.

The application acts as a GATT server defining service UUID D973F2E0-B19E-11E2-9E96-0800200C9A66

When using this service, the client sends requests to the characteristic D973F2E2-B19E-11E2-9E96-0800200C9A66, and gets notified on the characteristic D973F2E1-B19E-11E2-9E96-0800200C9A66 after registering for it.

Requests are encoded using the standard BLE 20 bytes MTU size

## Status Words

The following standard Status Words are returned for all APDUs - some specific Status Words can be used for specific commands and are mentioned in the command description.

'Status Words'

[width="80%"]
|===============================================================================================
| *SW*     | *Description*
|   6700   | Incorrect length
|   6982   | Security status not satisfied (Canceled by user)
|   6A80   | Invalid data
|   6B00   | Incorrect parameter P1 or P2
|   6Fxx   | Technical problem (Internal error, please report)
|   9000   | Normal ending of the command
|===============================================================================================
